# Analysis of process_auto_mouse()

## (1) Keycodes that Deactivate the Auto Mouse Layer

Non-mousekey presses trigger deactivation when auto mouse isn't active. The code states: "all non-mousekey presses restart delay timer and reset status" via `auto_mouse_reset_trigger()`. This function "turn the target layer off if on and reset toggle status" when a non-mousekey is pressed.

KC_TRNS and KC_NO are not explicitly handled in the switch statement, so they'd fall through to the default case and be treated as non-mousekey events.

## (2) QK_TO / TO(layer) Handling

```c
case QK_TO ... QK_TO_MAX:
    if (QK_TO_GET_LAYER(keycode) == (AUTO_MOUSE_TARGET_LAYER)) {
        if (!(record->event.pressed)) auto_mouse_toggle();
    }
    break;
```

TO keycodes targeting the auto mouse layer trigger `auto_mouse_toggle()` on key release only.

## (3) KC_TRNS and KC_NO Handling

Neither keycode receives explicit handling. Both fall to the default case, where they're checked via `is_mouse_record()`. If not mouse records, they invoke `auto_mouse_reset_trigger()` when not already active.

## (4) auto_mouse_layer_off() Function

```c
void auto_mouse_layer_off(void) {
    if (layer_state_is((AUTO_MOUSE_TARGET_LAYER)) && 
        (AUTO_MOUSE_ENABLED) && !layer_hold_check()) {
        layer_off((AUTO_MOUSE_TARGET_LAYER));
    }
}
```

This function disables the target layer conditionally. It modifies no internal context state—only the active layer. The function checks `is_enabled` implicitly through `AUTO_MOUSE_ENABLED` and respects the `is_toggled` flag via `layer_hold_check()`.

